  "worked_solution": "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=-3$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{\u2032}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(-3)+{f}^{\u2032}(-3)(x+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph of the function passes through the point $(-3,-4),$ so we have $f(-3)=-4.$ Also, we're told that ${f}^{\u2032}(-3)=3,$ so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =-4+3(x+3) \\\\  & =-4+3x+9 \\\\  & =3x+5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(-2.9)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=-2.9.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2.9) & \u2248L(-2.9) \\\\  & =3(-2.9)+5 \\\\  & =-8.7+5 \\\\  & =-3.7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the function is concave down on the interval $(-4,-2),$ the linear approximation $L(x)$ is above the graph near $x=-3,$ and therefore we have an overestimate.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
